From 8ab24d4b47adeb9897623627060846070d41219d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: vince <vincepaul.liem@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 14:31:29 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] feat(storage): share owned inode identity across hardlink
 names

Share bytes, permissions and link counts through a canonical VFS inode.
Retain named file and executable references independently so removed
alias entries cannot be recycled before their references retire. Preserve
shared duplicate offsets, fresh reopen offsets and whiteout identity.

Signed-off-by: vince <vincepaul.liem@gmail.com>
---
 README.md                                  |  12 +-
 crates/ginux-init/src/channel_hooks.rs     |   1 +
 crates/ginux-init/src/descriptors.rs       |   2 +-
 crates/ginux-init/src/device_hooks.rs      |   1 +
 crates/ginux-init/src/fs_hooks.rs          |  36 ++
 crates/ginux-init/src/tracee_io.rs         |   5 +-
 crates/ginux-vfs/src/lib.rs                | 398 +++++++++++++++++----
 scripts/test-launch-directories.py         |   6 +-
 scripts/test-launch-owned-exec.py          |   2 +-
 tests/fixtures/launch-directory-fixture.c  |  37 ++
 tests/fixtures/launch-owned-exec-fixture.c |  13 +-
 11 files changed, 441 insertions(+), 72 deletions(-)

diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 9fffd909..92f9a305 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -241,8 +241,16 @@ Namespace changes validate types, capacity and replacement before mutation.
 Relative AT_FDCWD paths follow the calling process's image cwd; owned directory
 FDs resolve relative operations through their current VFS identity. Image chdir,
 getcwd and relative image exec retain native per-process cwd semantics. Entering
-owned directories as cwd, relative image-directory FD access, hardlinks and
-guest file mappings remain unsupported.
+owned directories as cwd, relative image-directory FD access and guest file
+mappings remain unsupported.
+Owned regular-file link/linkat names share inode, bytes, permissions and link
+counts. Removing one name preserves data through other names, file descriptions
+and executable references. Descriptions and executable pins retain the name
+through which they were obtained, including rename and deletion state. Hardlinks
+consume namespace entries without duplicating file capacity. Only owned regular
+files are linkable; linkat supports flags zero or AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW. Symlink inode
+links, image-file imports, descriptor-only AT_EMPTY_PATH linking and directory
+hardlinks remain unsupported. Failed creation preserves names and link counts.
 Owned symlink/symlinkat and readlink/readlinkat support bounded targets and
 nofollow metadata. Link expansion precedes later parent components; mutations
 name the final link itself, including directory requirements on trailing slashes.
